Loss detection as quic-go does it for a client.

quic-go declares a packet lost once packetThreshold = 3 packets after it are acknowledged, not
counting packet numbers it skipped, or once it is older than 9/8 of the larger of the latest and the
smoothed RTT (detectLostPackets and Difference:
vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:827-911,
vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_history.go:248-267). Upstream ngtcp2 differed in
three ways a client shows:

  - its packet threshold grows with the flight, to half the packets in flight and up to 256: with
    a hundred packets out and the fiftieth acknowledged, quic-go declares 47 lost and ngtcp2
    declared none;
  - it declared the lost newest first, and their frames went back to the streams, and the streams
    into the retransmission queue, in that order; quic-go walks its history oldest first;
  - it had RFC 9002's persistent congestion, which quic-go does not: after a loss window of three
    PTOs it started the RTT estimate over and reset the controller.

A client now runs rtb_detect_lost_pkt_qgo; the server keeps upstream's code. With it, hysteria's
controller gets no congestion_event from loss detection: quic-go runs OnCongestionEvent per lost
packet in the same pass, which hybbr does with the ACK or the loss timer from on_pkt_lost, so the
only congestion_event left is ECN-CE — a no-op for hysteria's BBR
(vendor/hysteria/core/internal/congestion/bbr/bbr_sender.go:534-536) and a cutback at the largest
acknowledged packet for NewReno. Before, NewReno took every loss event as one, and cut back again
when a loss from an earlier window came with an ACK beyond it.

Checked against quic-go's own sentPacketHandler, run by tools/qgovec: test/ngpatch.

